# Cyrius Field Notes — Language Gotchas (diagnostics & fixed caps)
# Initialized-global-var slot ceiling, cc2 output buffer ceiling, preprocessed
# line-number reporting, the undefined-fn HARD ERROR (default-on since
# v6.3.2 — `--strict` no longer has anything to do with it), the LIVE
# capacity ceilings, and what a v6.4.60+ diagnostic actually looks like.
# Refreshed 2026-08-07 at v6.5.10 by RUNNING the compiler:
#  · `undefined_fn_warning_vs_strict` was inverted — a reachable undefined fn
#    has been a hard error BY DEFAULT since v6.3.2, `--allow-undef` downgrades
#    it, and `_strict_mode` is now assigned in all 7 forks and read NOWHERE.
#  · the transcripts here put the parenthetical note on its own line; the
#    compiler appends it to the `error:` line. Corrected in both entries.
#  · every capacity figure re-verified against `_capacity_warnings` and the
#    `src/main.cyr` heap map; the code-buffer warning fires at 75 %, not 85 %.
# v6.6.16: +1 entry type_name_refusals_v6616 (a non-type annotation is one named error;
# nine shapes compiled silently before).
# 7 entries (DERIVED at 6.6.16 with `grep -c '^\[\[entries\]\]'`).
# v6.7.7: +1 entry forward_call_arity_checked_v677 (a wrong-arity call to a fn defined later is an
# error, fork F2). 8 entries (DERIVED at 6.7.7).
# v6.7.8: updated type_name_refusals_v6616 (a trait member's signature is parsed and its type names
# checked; a slice outside a fn-local var names its position). 8 entries (DERIVED at 6.7.8).
# v6.7.11: +2 entries — a_kept_dead_fn_is_ud2_and_the_syms_map_predates_compaction_v6711 (DCE's kept dead bodies,
# the pre-compaction symbol map), a_compile_mode_switch_is_0_or_1_since_6_7_11 (`CYRIUS_POISON=0` was ON).
# 10 entries (DERIVED at 6.7.11).
# v6.7.12: live_compiler_capacity_ceilings — cx's code region is 4 MiB (off-heap, fixed, refused by name past it; D12).
# still 10 entries.
# v6.7.13: the capacity entry (the_gvar_wall) gains the `**T` pointee cap (126, D9). Still 10 entries.
# v6.7.14: +2 entries — a_diagnostic_names_a_method_and_an_instance_as_written_v6714 (`'S.m'` / `'g<i32>'`, the arity
# without `self`, FE-23), cyrius_ir_3_is_still_opt_in_v6714 (the arena, three IR=3 miscompiles fixed, one filed).
# Updated: live_compiler_capacity_ceilings (the `**T` pointee cap is 16383, VC-1; the CYRIUS_IR arena, IN-1 — the
# 6.7.13 line above put that cap in the_gvar_wall, it is in this entry), a_kept_dead_fn_is_ud2_and_the_syms_map_predates_
# compaction_v6711 (its two known defects fixed — CYRIUS_SYMS maps the compacted image, the pad is zeros; a live-holding
# dead fn is kept; no run cap; the dead count can rise; the name kept as the key). 12 entries (DERIVED at 6.7.14).

[[entries]]
name = "the_gvar_wall"
description = "The initialized global variable limit and how to work within it (refreshed v5.8.47 audit; cap raised pre-v5.8.x)"
category = "cyrius"
---
**Re-verified 2026-08-07 (v6.5.10)** against the `src/main.cyr` heap
map — offset and count both unchanged since the v6.4.2 pass below.

**Refreshed 2026-07-04 (v6.4.2 vidya audit)**: cap-bump history.
At cyrius v6.4.2 the gvar_toks table lives at heap offset
**0x729000** and holds **4096 entries** — relocated + grown from
1024@0x198000 at **v6.3.41** (thoth consumer unblock; the old
0x198000 band was FREED and reclaimed for the move, per the
src/main.cyr heap map). Only NON-literal top-level `var = expr;`
inits consume a gvar_toks slot; a bare int-literal `= NUM;` takes
the static-init fast path and enums const-fold — neither counts.
(History: 256 → 1024 across v5.6→v5.8 at 0x198000, → 4096 at
0x729000 at v6.3.41.) The MITIGATION STRATEGIES below are still
load-bearing for sandhi-class consumers + AGNOS, but the wall
itself moved far higher than the original DOOM-era writeup.
Misleading-error-on-undefined-var has long since been fixed
(undefined-var diagnostics name the variable explicitly across
all current backends). Companion entry
`undefined_fn_warning_vs_strict` covers the related
warning-vs-error escalation.

ORIGINAL (DOOM-era, kept for historical context):

THE WALL YOU WILL HIT:

The compiler tracks initialized global variables in a table (gvar_toks) at
heap offset 0x98000. The table holds 256 entries. Every `var x = value;` at
file scope consumes one slot. This includes your code AND every library you
include.

When we first tried to compile cyrius-doom with all modules, we thought the
limit was 64. The error message was `unexpected ';'` — completely unhelpful.
It took hours to discover the real cause was a missing variable (removed to
save slots) that another module still referenced.

cc2 v2.2.2 fixed the error message to `undefined variable 'player_ammo_bullets'`
and the fix took 30 seconds.

LESSON: When you hit a mysterious parse error in a large program, check if
you're referencing a variable that doesn't exist. The old compiler gives
misleading errors for this.

MITIGATION STRATEGIES:
1. Use enums for constants — enums don't consume gvar_toks slots:
     enum Screen { SCREEN_WIDTH = 320; SCREEN_HEIGHT = 200; }
   vs:
     var SCREEN_WIDTH = 320;    # costs a slot
     var SCREEN_HEIGHT = 200;   # costs another slot

2. Pack related mutable state into heap blocks:
     var tick_state = 0;   # one slot
     fn tick_init() { tick_state = alloc(24); }
     # [0]=count [8]=start_sec [16]=start_nsec
   vs:
     var tick_count = 0;      # three slots
     var tick_start_sec = 0;
     var tick_start_nsec = 0;

3. Don't include lib/io.cyr if you only need file_open/read/write/close.
   Inline them as one-line functions — saves 9 gvar_toks.

4. Count your globals before adding a new include. The stdlib contributes:
   string.cyr: 0, alloc.cyr: 3, fmt.cyr: 0, io.cyr: 9, args.cyr: 1, vec.cyr: 0

In our DOOM build: 64 globals across 9 modules + 4 stdlib files. Every
single one accounted for.

[[entries]]
name = "the_256kb_ceiling"
description = "Output-buffer ceiling and why static arrays destroy your binary size (refreshed v6.1.27; ceiling raised 256K→2M pre-v5.8.x→16M at v6.1.27; var buf[N] static-array semantics unchanged)"
category = "cyrius"
---
**CORRECTED 2026-08-07 (v6.5.10): the 16 MiB ceiling below is GONE.**
Since **v6.4.52** the output buffer is a **1 GiB off-heap allocation**
on every platform — `src/main.cyr:776` `_output_base =
alloc(1073741824)`, `_output_cap = 1073741824` — not a heap region at
all. There is no 16 MiB output ceiling to hit.

⛔ AND NOTE HOW THIS ENTRY WENT WRONG, because it is the failure this
whole file exists to prevent. An earlier pass on this same day stamped
it "re-verified against the `src/main.cyr` heap map — still 16 MiB,
unchanged". The heap map is a COMMENT, and that comment was itself
stale: it still documented `0x4D9D000 output_buf [16777216]` as live
while nothing had written there since v6.4.52. Verifying a doc against
another doc reproduces the error with a fresh timestamp on it, which is
worse than leaving it unstamped. Read the CODE.

**Refreshed 2026-06-10 (v6.1.27)**: ceiling moved.
At cyrius v6.1.27 the output_buf region is **16 MiB (16,777,216
bytes)** at S+0x4D9D000 (heap-top, per `src/main.cyr` heap map),
not the 256 KB cc2 ceiling described below. History: 1 MB →
2 MB at v5.6.43 (then at S+0x94A000), → 16 MB at v6.1.27 when
phylax hit the 2 MB ELF-output wall pulling bayan (relocated
mid-heap → heap-top; cost is virtual-only, output_buf is never
memset). The DOOM-era "950 KB of static arrays" example would
no longer overflow today, but the underlying RULE — heap-allocate anything
>~100 bytes — remains correct. Companion v5.8.46 fixed the
adjacent token-array cap (262144 → 1048576) which was binding
sandhi consumers like sit; see archived issue
`cyrius/docs/development/issues/archived/2026-04-25-cyrius-fixup-table-cap.md`
for that closeout.

ORIGINAL (DOOM-era, kept for historical context):

cc2 caps output at 262,144 bytes (256KB). Both the code buffer and the final
ELF are limited to this size.

Critical: `var buf[N]` allocates N bytes IN THE BINARY. Not on the heap. Not
as BSS. The compiler zero-initializes every byte and writes it to the ELF.

Our first DOOM build had:
  var screen_buf[64000];    # 64KB in binary
  var rgb_buf[256000];      # 256KB — ALONE exceeds the limit
  var map_nodes[114688];    # 112KB
  var map_segs[196608];     # 192KB
  var things[57344];        # 56KB

Total: ~950KB of static arrays. Compiler error: "output too large (3617920/262144)".

THE FIX: Heap allocate everything:
  var screen_buf = 0;       # 8 bytes in binary
  fn framebuf_init() {
      screen_buf = alloc(64000);   # 64KB at runtime
  }

After converting all large arrays to heap: 56KB binary. The data lives in
the 1MB heap that alloc_init() sets up via brk.

RULE: If it's bigger than ~100 bytes, heap-allocate it. Static arrays are
for small stack buffers (var buf[16]) inside functions only.

[[entries]]
name = "the_preprocessed_line_number_problem"
description = "Error messages report preprocessed line numbers, not source file + line — STILL ACTIVE at v5.8.46 (re-confirmed at v5.8.47 vidya audit; partially mitigated by the lex.cyr file_map but not surfaced in the error path)"
category = "cyrius"
---
**Status (2026-08-07, v6.5.10 — re-run, unchanged)**: PARTIALLY
MITIGATED. The line number is still the PREPROCESSED-stream line and
the filename still collapses to literal `<source>` — but since v6.4.60
the diagnostic also carries a **column** and prints the **offending
source line with a caret under it**, which in practice removes most of
the counting work below:

    $ echo 'var iv_add = 1;' | build/cycc > /dev/null
    error:<source>:1:5: expected identifier, got reserved keyword 'iv_add' (cannot be used as an identifier; rename the variable/field/fn)
        var iv_add = 1;
            ^

You can usually just read the excerpt and grep for it. See
`diagnostics_carry_column_excerpt_and_dont_stop_at_the_first_error`
in this file. The underlying file+line tracking is still unshipped.

**Prior status (2026-05-03)**: still active at cyrius v5.8.46.
Empirical re-test: a 5-line program with `var x = SOME_UNDEFINED;`
on line 4 errors as `error:<source>:2: undefined variable
'SOME_UNDEFINED'` — the line number doesn't match the user
source AND the source filename collapses to literal `<source>`
even when invoked through `cyrius build` (which has the real
filename available). Partial mitigation infrastructure exists
(`file_map` + `file_map_str` regions in `src/frontend/lex.cyr`,
ipc'd via `_resolve_file_line` in some error paths) but the
common error sites still emit `error:<source>:N:` with the
preprocessed-stream line number.

ORIGINAL (DOOM-era, kept for historical context):

When cc2 reports `error:1984: unexpected ';'`, that 1984 is the line number
in the PREPROCESSED source — after all includes have been expanded into a
single stream.

With 5 stdlib includes (485 lines) + 9 DOOM modules (2500 lines), you get
line numbers in the thousands that don't correspond to any single file.

TO FIND THE REAL LOCATION:
1. Count cumulative lines through your include order
2. Subtract from the error line number
3. That gives you the offset into the specific file

Example: error at preprocessed line 1984
  string.cyr: 85
  alloc.cyr: 103
  fmt.cyr: 216
  args.cyr: 81
  fixed.cyr: 85
  tables.cyr: 114
  wad.cyr: 183
  framebuf.cyr: 149
  map.cyr: 338
  render.cyr: 367
  (cumulative: 1721)
  1984 - 1721 = line 263 in the next file (input.cyr)

This is tedious. The fix is in cc2's roadmap: track source file + line
through preprocessing and report both.

WORKAROUND: Binary search. Comment out includes until the error goes away,
then narrow down. This is faster than counting.

[[entries]]
name = "undefined_fn_warning_vs_strict"
description = "⚠ INVERTED SINCE v6.3.2 — a REACHABLE undefined-function call is a HARD ERROR by DEFAULT and no binary is emitted; `--allow-undef` downgrades it to a warning. `--strict` is NOT what turns it on any more (and is now read nowhere). Entry name kept for link stability."
category = "cyrius"
---
⚠ **CORRECTED 2026-08-07 (v6.5.10) — the default flipped at v6.3.2 and
this entry taught the OPPOSITE for two minors.** Measured on the live
compiler, no flags:

    $ printf 'var r = bench_print_all();\n' | build/cycc > foo
    warning: undefined function 'bench_print_all'
    warning: undefined function 'bench_print_all' (reachable call site)
    error: refusing to emit binary with 1 reachable undefined function(s) (pass --allow-undef to downgrade)
    $ echo $? ; stat -c%s foo
    1
    0

(Until cycc 6.6.10 the second line read "(call site may be unreachable)" —
INVERTED: it is printed only for a call site the reachability filter judged
REACHABLE, which is exactly why the refusal follows it. The first, bare line
is the pre-pass warning and fires for every reference, reachable or not;
match on that prefix if you parse these.)

**No binary. Zero bytes. Exit 1 — by default.** That closes the "ships a
successful build of a crashing binary" class: the old behaviour emitted a
`ud2` / `UDF` at the call site, which SIGILLs at runtime and was
repeatedly misdiagnosed as a toolchain bug.

⛔ **`--strict` IS NOT THE SWITCH, and does nothing here.** The gate reads
a separate `_allow_undef` global (default 0 = hard error), not
`_strict_mode`. Measured: `build/cycc --strict` and `build/cycc` produce
byte-identical output on the case above. `_strict_mode` is still declared
in six of the seven `src/main*.cyr` forks (not `main_cx.cyr`) and
assigned by their argv parses, and is **read nowhere** in `src/` — the
remaining mentions are comments. Do not add `--strict` to CI expecting it
to do this; it is already done.

TO OPT OUT (rarely correct):

    cycc --allow-undef      # or --al

Downgrades the error back to a warning and emits the binary. The repo's
own builds use **zero** `--allow-undef`; every reachable undef in the
tree is a real fn via includes, or a documented stub/guard.

⚠ **"REACHABLE" IS LOAD-BEARING.** The check runs AFTER the DCE pass and
consults the reachability bitmap, so a fixup whose host fn is unreachable
from entry warns but does NOT count toward the error (v5.11.59). That is
why you can see a `warning: undefined function 'X'` line and still get a
binary — the two `warning:` lines above are the same symbol reported by
both paths. Top-level / entry-block code is always treated as live.

THE BUG CLASS IT CATCHES is typo-in-fn-name, forgotten include, and API
rename without the caller updated. Surfaced at v5.4.15 when
`bench_print_all` (should have been `bench_report_all`) cost a full debug
cycle because the warning scrolled past in the compile output. Nothing
scrolls past now.

HISTORY, for anyone on an older toolchain: warn-and-emit was the default
through v6.3.1; `--strict` (v5.4.19) was the opt-in hard-fail, and
`cyrius build --strict` forwarded it (v5.11.63). v6.3.2 made the hard
error unconditional and moved the escape hatch to `--allow-undef`.

[[entries]]
name = "live_compiler_capacity_ceilings"
description = "The capacity ceilings a consumer can actually hit — re-verified live at 6.6.20 against `_capacity_warnings`, parse_fn.cyr, lex.cyr and the drivers: fn_table init 2048 growing to a 131072 hard ceiling, identifier pool 8 MiB at 0xEC00000, var_table 8192 growing to 1,048,576, fixup_table 1,048,576 growing to 64M entries, string_data 2097152, code buffer 8 MiB growing to 64 MiB (4 MiB on cx since 6.7.12, off-heap and fixed; 512 KiB before), preprocessed source 24 MB, `**T` pointee types 16383 (126 in 6.7.13), and since 6.7.14 the CYRIUS_IR arena growing from 262,144 nodes to a 64M-node runaway ceiling. Read this instead of any older 262144 / 524288 / 32768-ceiling figure."
category = "cyrius"
---
THE LIVE NUMBERS (source of truth: `src/common/util.cyr::_capacity_warnings`
plus the `CYRIUS_STATS=1` meter in `src/main.cyr` — go read them, do not
trust a doc that hasn't been premise-checked. Every line below was
re-read out of the code on 2026-10-06 at the 6.6.20 closeout; the
v6.5.10 pass's numbers had gone 4-16x stale behind v6.5.40 / v6.5.51):

    fn_table        2048 INITIAL (v6.5.51), doubles to a 131072 HARD
                    ceiling (v6.5.40; parse_fn.cyr `too many functions`)
    identifier pool 8,388,608 bytes (8 MiB) at 0xEC00000 (v6.5.40);
                    LEX refuses at np + need >= 8,388,336
    var_table       8192 INITIAL, doubles to a hard 1,048,576 (v6.3.0)
    fixup_table     1,048,576 INITIAL, doubles to a 67,108,864-entry
                    runaway ceiling (v6.2.0; the warning still divides
                    by 1,048,576)
    string_data     2097152
    code buffer     8 MiB off-heap, +8 MiB steps to 67108864 (64 MiB,
                    v6.4.49)
                    — but only 4,194,304 (4 MiB, an off-heap alloc,
                      `_CX_CODE_CAP`) on the cx target since 6.7.12 (it
                      was 524288, 512 KiB, until then), which is NOT
                      growable: past it cycc_cx refuses by name ("cx
                      bytecode exceeds the cx code region (4194304 B):
                      … build this program for a native target"), and
                      the warning reads "% of the cx code region
                      (n/4194304 bytes)". cxvm loads it into an 8 MiB
                      guest memory (D12).
    preprocessed    25,165,824 (24 MB, `_SRC_CAP`, v6.5.40)
    output buffer   1073741824 (1 GiB, off-heap alloc, since v6.4.52)
    gvar_toks       4096 deferred non-literal global inits, at 0x729000
    `**T` pointees  16383 distinct pointee types of a pointer to a
                    pointer since 6.7.14 (VC-1: a 14-bit pin, interned
                    per STRUCT in the alloc()'d, doubling `_pp_tab`) —
                    past it an error, "too many pointer-to-pointer
                    pointee types (max 16383)", never a silent word.
                    6.7.13 (its D9) capped it at 126 with a 7-bit pin,
                    so 127 two-word structs through `**S` were refused
                    — programs 6.7.12 compiled. (Struct definitions
                    themselves cap at 1024: "too many struct
                    definitions (max 1024)".)
    IR arena        only under CYRIUS_IR (6.7.14, IN-1): one alloc()'d
                    arena taken when IR recording switches on (a default
                    build never takes it), 262,144 nodes / 32,768 blocks
                    initially, doubling; runaway ceilings "IR node table
                    exceeds 64M nodes" / "IR block table exceeds 16M
                    blocks" (and "cannot allocate the IR arena
                    (CYRIUS_IR)"). The old fixed caps — "IR node buffer
                    full" (1,048,576 nodes) / "IR block table full"
                    (131,072) — are gone: a CYRIUS_IR build of anything
                    the default build accepts succeeds.

HOW TO SEE YOUR OWN NUMBERS:

    CYRIUS_STATS=1 cyrius build ...      # full meter on stderr

  Without `CYRIUS_STATS`, the compiler emits an over-cap WARNING per
  table at end of compile. Since v6.4.50 all SEVEN drivers share one
  implementation — before that, only the x86-Linux and Win64 drivers
  warned at all, which is why a codebuf warning would appear on x86 and
  vanish on aarch64/macho/cx for the same program.

  ⚠ The threshold is **85 % for five of the six tables and 75 % for the
  code buffer** — a detail this entry used to flatten into "an 85 %
  warning per table". If you are calibrating against the warning, use
  the right number for the table you are near.

TWO TRAPS IN THE OLDER DOCUMENTATION:

  1. **"identifier buffer 262144" / "524288"** — 262144 was retired at
     v6.4.76, which doubled the pool 256 KB → 512 KB in place (into the
     free 0xA0000-0x100000 band; LEX's guard 261872 → 524016). The pool
     END then had to stay <= 0x100000 — past that physical ceiling the
     compiler HUNG — so v6.5.40 moved it out of the band entirely: 8 MiB
     at 0xEC00000, LEX guard **8,388,336**.

  2. **"fn_table cap 8192"** — 8192 was the INITIAL cap until v6.5.51
     (2048 since) and is still the var_table INITIAL cap. What changed
     is the ceiling and the warning:
     - v6.2.0 made the fn tables growable (REGFN doubles + rehashes).
     - v6.4.75 fixed a **P0**: six fn-INDEXED side tables had never been
       migrated and were still pinned to fixed 8192-slot bands, so past
       8192 fns index `fi` wrote into slot `fi-8192` of its NEIGHBOUR —
       silent metadata corruption, no diagnostic. All six now lazy-alloc
       at the ceiling (32768 then; `alloc(131072*8)` today).
     - The WARNING was complicit: it divided by the LIVE `_fnt_cap`, so
       it screamed "99%" at 8191 fns and went **SILENT** the moment the
       table doubled (9000/16384 = 54%) — precisely when the corruption
       began. It now divides by the fixed ceiling (32768 then, 131072
       since v6.5.40), which does not move.
     A blanket 8192 → 32768 find-and-replace INTRODUCES bugs. Check each
     site.

WARN THRESHOLDS (integer arithmetic — `count * 100 / cap >= 85`, and
`>= 75` for the code buffer): fn_table fires at 111,412 fns (of
131072); identifier pool at 7,130,317 bytes (of 8,388,608).

WHAT TO DO WHEN YOU HIT ONE: the standing rule is grow the cap, not
trim the program. Past the 131072-fn ceiling (`error: too many functions
(max 131072)`, parse_fn.cyr) the honest answer is splitting into
compilation units. The known high-water consumer is **stiva**, which at
v6.4.73 sat ~750 fns below the then-**8192** cliff — i.e. ~7,400 fns,
about 6 % of today's 131072 ceiling. ⚠ That distance has NOT been
re-measured since the ceiling moved; read it as "the biggest consumer we
know of is nowhere near the current ceiling", not as a live margin.
(v6.4.x history: this paragraph read "past 32768 fns" and "~23 % of
today's 32768 ceiling" — 32768 was the `_fnt_grow` ceiling from v6.2.0
until v6.5.40 — and said the identifier pool "no longer has room to grow in
place", which was true of its 0x60000 placement capped below 0x100000;
v6.5.40 moved the pool to 8 MiB at 0xEC00000, see trap 1 above.)

[[entries]]
name = "diagnostics_carry_column_excerpt_and_dont_stop_at_the_first_error"
description = "Since v6.4.60 every error/warning prints `line:COLUMN` plus the source line with a caret; since v6.4.62 one compile reports MANY errors (panic-mode recovery), writes NO output file if any error fired, and cannot hang on hostile or truncated input."
category = "cyrius"
---
WHAT YOU SEE NOW (both transcripts re-run at v6.5.10 — the
parenthetical note is APPENDED to the `error:` line, it is not on a
line of its own; an earlier revision of this entry showed it wrapped):

    $ echo 'var iv_add = 1;' | cycc > /dev/null
    error:<source>:1:5: expected identifier, got reserved keyword 'iv_add' (cannot be used as an identifier; rename the variable/field/fn)
        var iv_add = 1;
            ^

    $ printf 'var a: f64 = f64_from(3);\nvar b = a + 1;\n' | cycc > /dev/null
    warning:<source>:2:14: f64 arithmetic with a non-f64 right operand

THREE BEHAVIOURS WORTH RELYING ON (all three re-measured at v6.5.10 —
two missing-`;` errors reported in ONE compile from a two-fn file, a
0-byte output with exit 1, and a source truncated mid-expression
terminating in 2 stderr lines rather than hanging):

  1. **Many errors per compile.** A syntax error puts the parser into
     panic mode; it skips to the next statement boundary and continues.
     One run gives you the batch instead of one error per edit-compile
     cycle. Scope is the `ERR_EXPECT` syntax class (346 sites); about 25
     inline fatal errors still stop on the first hit.
  2. **No output file when any error fired.** Every fork (including cx)
     gates its writer on `_had_error`. A **0-byte output** is the
     failure signature — if you see one, look at stderr, don't debug the
     binary. You can never accidentally run a stale artifact.
  3. **It cannot hang.** A `_had_error`-gated watchdog inside `PEEKT`
     aborts cleanly if the token index stalls past a bound far above any
     legal recursive-descent depth, and `PEEKT` clamps to EOF past the
     token count (v6.4.78). A truncated file used to emit 166,670 error
     lines; it now emits 5. On VALID input none of this machinery runs,
     so there is no cost to correct programs.

READING THE COLUMN: it points at the CURRENT parse token. For a
backward-looking check ("expected `;`") that is the token AFTER the one
you want — trust the caret over the number in those cases. A tab counts
as one column. There is no per-file source line yet (see
`the_preprocessed_line_number_problem`); the excerpt is what makes the
message findable in practice — grep the printed line.

IMPLEMENTATION NOTE (for anyone tempted to "improve" it): the column
costs ZERO new heap — the token byte offset is packed into the HIGH 32
bits of the existing `tok_lines` entry. And the watchdog is cheap for
the same reason the EOF clamp was cheap: `PEEKT` already had an
`_had_error` guard, and the runaway is post-error only, so clamping
inside it measured at −0.07 % on self_compile.

[[entries]]
name = "type_name_refusals_v6616"
description = "Every type name is resolved whole and a non-type is refused once, by name — including the shapes that compiled silently before 6.6.16"
category = "cyrius"
---
WHAT CHANGED (6.6.16). These all compiled rc 0 with no diagnostic before:
    var a: Nonexist = 3;            # a silent i64
    var a: i8x = 300;               # an i8 by PREFIX — read 44
    var p: *Nonexist = 0;           # local and global
    var s: [Nonexist] = 0;          # and slice<Nonexist>, [*Nonexist]: width 8
    fn f(a: Nonexist) / fn f(a: i16q)
    fn f(): (i64, Nonexist)
Each is now one `error:` naming the type (see language/core.cyml
`type_names_v6616` for the messages and the vocabulary), reported AT the name.

HOW IT STAYS ONE ERROR:
  - `_tn_refuse` clears the panic latch it set, so the declaration parses on
    as a plain i64: `fn f(a: Nope1, b: Nope2): Nope3` is three errors, and
    `sizeof(N1) + sizeof(N2)` two — not one plus a resync that eats the rest.
  - A generic fn's body is parsed for its base and once per instance; a
    per-token byte map (`_tn_once`) reports each name the first time only.
    (Before, an unknown array element in a generic body printed once per
    instance.)
  - An `#assert sizeof(Nope)` operand latches the panic as the old error did,
    so the #assert does not add `#assert failed`.

STILL ACCEPTED: Str/cstring/Result/Option/Tagged params by name; enums on
either side; a fn's type parameters (first, second, a THIRD, and all of them
under CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0 — read from the `<..>` tokens); a struct declared
below a local or param that names it.

If a downstream build newly fails with `unknown type`, the annotation names
nothing: a typo, a missing include, or a C type (`ptr`, `usize`). Fix the
annotation — or drop it (an unannotated var is an i64).

6.7.8 — TWO MORE PLACES ARE READ. A trait member's signature was never
parsed (only its parameter count, for the impl check), so these compiled:
    trait T { fn m(self, s: Nonexist): i64; }       # unknown type 'Nonexist' for parameter 's'
    trait T { fn m(self, n + foo bar): i64; }       # expected ')', got '+'
    trait T { fn m(self): i64 junk; }               # a trait method declaration ends with ';'
                                                    #   (required) or a body (a default)
— also in a default method every impl overrides. And a slice type outside
a fn-local `var` names its position instead of a misleading parse error:
    fn f(s: [u8])     # a slice cannot be a parameter (6.7.8: slices are fn-locals) - pass &s and read
                      #   slice_ptr / slice_len, or the pointer and the length
(was `expected ')', got identifier 'u8'`; `slice<u8>` was "unknown type
'slice'"). Gates: traits_checked.sh TS rows, slice_position_refused.sh.

[[entries]]
name = "forward_call_arity_checked_v677"
description = "Since cycc 6.7.7 a wrong-arity call to a fn defined LATER is the same error a call below the definition gets. Before, such a call from inside a fn body built with a surplus argument dropped or a missing one bound to whatever its register held."
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc 6.7.6 and older:

    fn caller(): i64 { var r = g(1, 2); return r; }   # g takes one argument
    fn g(a): i64 { return a; }
    # compiled clean: the 2 was dropped; g() with too FEW arguments read whatever its register held

The v6.5.1 arity check skipped any callee whose body was not yet emitted. A top-level STATEMENT's call to a fn
below it was already checked (that fn's body is emitted first); the gap was a call from inside a fn body, or to
a fn inside a later top-level block or a later include — a plain call, `o.m(..)`, `return f(..)`, an operator.

SINCE 6.7.7 (fork F2, user 2026-10-09) every call to a fn pass 1 recorded is checked:

    error: … 'g' expects 1 argument, got 2

A fn with parameter defaults takes a range ("'f' expects 1 to 3 arguments, got 4"). A name no definition
registers still ends in the undefined-function diagnostic. Over cyrius's own 853-file corpus, the 7 forks and the
self-compile, only the filed issue's two repros changed: a consumer meets it as a new error at a call that was
already wrong. Gate: tests/gates/frontend/default_named_args_checked.sh rows R24 / R24b, C1-fwd. See CHANGELOG
[6.7.7].

[[entries]]
name = "a_kept_dead_fn_is_ud2_and_the_syms_map_predates_compaction_v6711"
description = "Under CYRIUS_DCE=1 a dead fn the compiler keeps — one holding an instruction it cannot size (an `in` / `out` in `asm`), or since 6.7.14 one whose code range holds a live fn's start — stays in the image with `ud2; ud2; nop` where it called a removed fn: dead, not a miscompile. Since 6.7.14 CYRIUS_SYMS lists the COMPACTED image's addresses (it predated compaction through 6.7.13, which the entry's name records) and `.text`'s pad after the moved code is zeros; and the dead-fn count can rise against 6.7.13 for the same source — re-measure, do not pin it."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.11", "v6.7.14", "CYRIUS_DCE", "dead code", "compaction", "ud2", "CYRIUS_SYMS", "crash address", "generics"]
---
A KEPT DEAD FN DISASSEMBLES AS `ud2; ud2; nop` (6.7.11). Compaction removes a dead fn only when its length decoder can
size every instruction in it; one holding an `in` / `out` in an `asm` block is KEPT, and its calls / tail calls into
removed fns become `ud2; ud2; nop` (its `&fn` of a removed fn reads 0). It is dead — nothing calls it — so this is not a
miscompile; any other rel32 from it into removed code refuses the build by name. Since 6.7.14 (IN-5) a dead fn whose
code range holds a LIVE fn's start is kept whole too, and still reported unreachable: a generic instance is emitted
inside the fn that first needs it, so a dead forwarder can contain a live instance — through 6.7.13 the sweep removed
the live body with it (x86 SIGILL 132, an aarch64 hang, a PE fault).

`CYRIUS_SYMS` UNDER `CYRIUS_DCE=1` MAPS THE BINARY IT SHIPS BESIDE since 6.7.14 (IN-8). Through 6.7.13 it was written
before compaction: a removed fn appeared at a live fn's address and every later fn sat high by the removed bytes, so a
crash address resolved to the wrong function (this entry's name records that defect; it is kept as the key). Now it
lists the compacted addresses, without the removed fns, and a kept dead fn where it sits: resolve a crash address
against the map of the binary that crashed. And `.text`'s alignment pad after the compacted code is zeros since 6.7.14
(IN-3; it kept stale pre-compaction bytes that read like a miscompile in a disassembly) — after `CYRIUS_IR=3`'s
compaction too.

NO DEAD-FN COUNT MAKES COMPACTION GIVE UP (6.7.14, IN-4). Through 6.7.13 more than 4,096 dead-code runs declined the
whole compaction ("compaction declined on x86_64 ELF: more than 4096 dead-code runs, the repair registry cap"); a
decline now means only that an allocation failed ("… could not grow (alloc failed)"). A redefined fn's losing body keeps
nothing alive: what only it calls is removed (IN-6). ⚠ RE-MEASURE, DO NOT PIN THE COUNT: since 6.7.14 the x86 call
graph counts a `call` / `jmp rel32` only where an instruction STARTS (IN-18 — it counted any E8 byte whose rel32 landed
on a fn start, so the dead set moved when an unrelated global was added), so a `CYRIUS_DCE=1` "N unreachable fns" note
or `dead:` list can be longer than 6.7.13's for the same source. CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (INF-5), [6.7.14] IN-3 – IN-6, IN-8,
IN-18.

[[entries]]
name = "a_compile_mode_switch_is_0_or_1_since_6_7_11"
description = "`CYRIUS_POISON=0` meant ON until 6.7.11 (any set value — `0`, empty, a word — turned the poisoned allocator on). Since 6.7.11 the three compile-mode switches agree: CYRIUS_POISON, CYRIUS_BOUNDS and CYRIUS_COVERAGE take `1` for on; `0`, empty or unset for off; a word (`yes`, `true`, `no`) stops the build by name. On Windows (cmd.exe, wine) an empty variable is unset."
category = "cyrius"
---
    CYRIUS_POISON=0 cyrius build x.cyr build/x       # 6.7.10: POISON ON. 6.7.11: off
    CYRIUS_POISON=yes cyrius build x.cyr build/x     # 6.7.11: error: CYRIUS_POISON must be 0 or 1 (or unset): yes

The rule is decided once, in the shared preprocessor (before the first `#ifdef`), for all three switches — every fork
reads the same answer. `cyrius fuzz --poison` hands the compiler `CYRIUS_POISON=1` itself. `cyrius pulsar` and the
on-demand cx builds strip the three switches (and `CYRIUS_COVERAGE_FILE`) so a tracked compiler is never built in a
mode; your own compiles keep them. To scrub any OTHER variable read raw, `unset` it (`V=` keeps it set and empty).
CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (TL-8, TL-9).

[[entries]]
name = "a_diagnostic_names_a_method_and_an_instance_as_written_v6714"
description = "Since cycc 6.7.14 a call-site diagnostic names a method `S.m` and a generic call `g<i32>` as the source spells them — never the mangled `S_m` / `T_Tr_m` / `g$i32` — and a method call's arity counts the arguments in its parentheses, without `self`. Grep for `'S.m'` / `'g<i32>'`. A generic METHOD's generics refusals still say `P_pick` (filed)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "diagnostics", "methods", "generics", "arity", "mangled names", "#deprecated", "#must_use", "#pure", "grep"]
---
    s.two(1, 2)     # fn two(self, a) — 6.7.14: 'S.two' expects 1 argument, got 2   (6.7.13: 'S_two' expects 2 arguments, got 3)
    s.ri(b)         # 6.7.14: … in a call to 'S.ri' - pass &b for its address      (6.7.13: 'S_ri')
    s.nope(1)       # 6.7.14: undefined function 'S.nope'                         (6.7.13: 'S_nope')

A diagnostic at a call — arity, named arguments, the struct-type and vector-argument refusals, `#pure`, `#deprecated`,
`#must_use`, `private`, the multi-value destructure, the bool / cstring parameter checks, "undefined function" — names
a method `S.m` (a trait-qualified one `T.m`, not `T_Tr_m`; an inferred generic method `P.id<f64>`) and a generic call
`g<i32>` (`g2<i32, i64>`, `gx<Box<i64>>`, `g<f64v2>`) as the source spells them (FE-23), and a struct-argument
refusal names a method result `'Pt.dup'` (VC-11). Through 6.7.13 they printed the mangled `S_m` / `T_Tr_m` and the
instance `g$i32`. A free fn named `S_x` (no impl) and a `mod` fn `mm_f` keep their names.

A METHOD CALL'S ARITY COUNTS THE ARGUMENTS IN ITS PARENTHESES, WITHOUT `self` — a `dyn` call likewise (`d.show(1)` on
`show(self)`: "expects 0 arguments, got 1"). The free spelling `S_two(&s, 1, 2)` is named `S.two` and counts its `&s`
("'S.two' expects 2 arguments, got 3"), and a method declaring no parameter at all keeps the receiver in its count
(`g.zero()` on `fn zero()`: "'MG.zero' expects 0 arguments, got 1").

So a script or a test that greps diagnostics greps `'S.m'` and `'g<i32>'`, and a build log's warnings that name a method
or an instance (`#deprecated`, `#must_use`, `#pure`, "undefined function" under `--allow-undef`) read the new spelling.
Nothing compiles differently. ⚠ The generics refusals that name a generic METHOD keep its mangled name — "generic
'P_pick' has no i64 … instance", "generic 'P_bm': its type argument does not implement trait 'Show'" (filed, 6.7.15).
Instance NAMES in the coverage dump and `cyrius syms` stay `$`-mangled (semantics_runtime.cyml generics_since_6_7_14).
CHANGELOG [6.7.14] FE-23, VC-11.

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_ir_3_is_still_opt_in_v6714"
description = "`CYRIUS_IR=3` (the IR optimisation pipeline) is opt-in and not release-grade in 6.7.14: a const fold over a dead store NOP-fills its own immediate (filed, 6.7.15). 6.7.14 removed its storage caps (an IR build of anything the default build accepts succeeds) and fixed three miscompiles (a signed narrow field read, a field read after a dead store, a float literal). Measure with it; ship the default pipeline."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "CYRIUS_IR", "IR=3", "optimizer", "LASE", "DSE", "const fold", "opt-in", "miscompile"]
---
    fn f(): i64 { var r = 0; r = r + 10; r = r + 100; return r; }   # CYRIUS_IR=3: faults (filed, 6.7.15) — default: 110

`CYRIUS_IR=3` runs the IR passes (LASE, const fold, DCE, DSE) over the emitted x86 code. It is an opt-in measuring mode,
and no release build uses it. What 6.7.14 changed under it:
  - THE STORAGE (IN-1): the IR is one `alloc()`'d arena that grows (live_compiler_capacity_ceilings), so "IR node buffer
    full" / "IR block table full" are gone — an IR build of anything the default build accepts succeeds (a 3.0 MB
    program was refused under IR=1 / IR=3 through 6.7.13).
  - THREE MISCOMPILES FIXED: a signed narrow field read through a pointer or a capture added the field's ADDRESS, a field
    read right after a dead store read through a stale register (IN-2), and a float literal let LASE keep a stale
    register across it (`q.f = 2.5;` through a pointer stored through 2.5's bits; ST-1). Each came from an emitter that
    recorded no IR node: the passes NOP-fill a node's byte range, and bytes nobody recorded belong to the node before
    them.
  - ⚠ STILL OPEN (filed, 6.7.15): a const fold over a dead store NOP-fills its own immediate — the fn above faults; one
    corpus file (`derive_enum_inside_ifdef`) exits 139 under IR=3.
So use `CYRIUS_IR=3` to measure, and ship the default pipeline; `CYRIUS_DCE_CAP=0` / `CYRIUS_DSE_CAP=0` narrow down which
pass misbehaves. CHANGELOG [6.7.14] IN-1, IN-2, IN-3, ST-1.
